        "id": "ch2_2-1",
        "text": "Files, directories, and permission commands\nFiles and directories are organized in a hierarchical structure, with the root directory as the top-most\nlevel. Each file and directory has a set of permissions that determine who can read, write, or execute\nit. The file permissions are divided into three categories\u2014user, group, and others:\n\u2022 user : This refers to the owner of the file\n\u2022 group :  This refers to a group of users assigned to the file (e.g., developers)\n\u2022 others : This refers to anyone else who is not the owner or part of the assigned group\nLet\u2019s take a look at the hierarchical structure:\nFigure 2.14 \u2013 Linux files and directory hierarchical structure\nTo manage file and directory permissions in Linux, there are several command line tools available.\nSome of the commonly used commands include chmod , chown , and chgrp .\nFiles, directories, and permission commands\n41\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 chmod\n\u2022 chown\n\u2022 chgrp\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
